I'm going to call BS on this point here.
Why does it have to be {da}?

We say "everybody", which semantically restricts it to people.
We say "every day", which semantically restricts it to days.
We say "everything", which means everything. Absolutely everything.

{ro prenu} or {ro djedi} or {ro da}... not semantically difficult and not a=
=20
huge mouthful. {roda} is fine exactly as it is and means what it does. We d=
o=20
-not- need to generalise. If I have to stop and ask, "Wait, did you mean=20
-everything- everything or 'everything we're talking about' everything?"=20
then I don't want to speak Lojban anymore. Seriously. That is -THE-=20
definitive point of why I speak and learn Lojban. One word, one meaning.=20
{roda} means everything. Everything everything. All things. That is what it=
=20
literally means. If we say that it has an implicit NOI clause that provides=
=20
context, then absolutely -every- member of KOhA also has to have an implici=
t=20
NOI clause. This is Not A Good Thing=99.

I veto your laziness and suggest that you say what you mean.

